**The most important judgement is that the pilot assumptions produce an operating loss: the current pre-feasibility production and economic case needs revision before an investment decision.**

(a) Annual recovered gold is:

\[
\text{Recovered oz/yr}=\frac{\text{bcm/h}\times6{,}000\times0.35\times\text{recovery}}{31.1035}
\]

- **Design case:** annual throughput \(=200\times6{,}000=1{,}200{,}000\) bcm.

\[
\frac{1{,}200{,}000\times0.35\times0.85}{31.1035}
=\frac{357{,}000}{31.1035}
=\boxed{11{,}477.81\text{ oz/yr}}
\]

- **Pilot case:** annual throughput \(=110\times6{,}000=660{,}000\) bcm.

\[
\frac{660{,}000\times0.35\times0.72}{31.1035}
=\frac{166{,}320}{31.1035}
=\boxed{5{,}347.31\text{ oz/yr}}
\]

(b) Using the unrounded pilot ounces:

\[
\text{Annual revenue}=5{,}347.30818\times2{,}400
=\boxed{\text{US\$12.83354 M}}
\]

\[
\text{Annual operating margin}=12.83354-13.0
=\boxed{-\text{US\$0.16646 M}}
\]

That is approximately **US$166,460 annual operating loss**, before capital recovery and any other costs excluded from the stated operating cost.

\[
\text{Nominal resource processing life}
=\frac{2{,}400{,}000}{660{,}000}
=\boxed{3.63636\text{ years}}
\]

This assumes the entire resource is accessible and processed at the stated grade, without mining losses or dilution. The final year is partial.

(c) Throughput is **45% below design**, and recovery is **13 percentage points below design**. Together, these reduce annual recovered gold by **53.4%**. With annual operating cost largely fixed, extending the processing life from 2.0 to 3.64 years adds operating expenditure and delays revenue.

The fines content and clay balls provide a credible explanation for the underperformance. Inadequate disaggregation can leave gold trapped within clay aggregates; fines can restrict screening capacity and impair jig separation. These mechanisms require confirmation through a reconciled gold balance, including assays of clay balls, coarse rejects and fine tailings. The 25% fines figure is a **mass proportion**, so it should not be applied as a further volumetric or gold deduction.

The next technical gate is representative testing across gravel domains and clay variability, followed by demonstrated scrubbing and separation improvements and updated capital and operating estimates. Three trenches provide limited spatial coverage. Operating breakeven at 110 bcm/h requires approximately **72.93% recovery**, but merely reaching that threshold would leave no operating surplus to repay capital or reward risk. The Indicated resource alone does not establish an economically mineable reserve.

