**The single most important judgement: the slide is unacceptable because the Mineral Resources already include the Ore Reserves; adding them double-counts inventory.**

With tonnes expressed in Mt:
\[
\text{Contained gold (koz)}=\frac{\text{Mt}\times\text{grade (g/t)}\times1{,}000}{31.1035}
\]

**(a) Measured plus Indicated Resource**
\[
\frac{[(6.0\times1.80)+(14.0\times1.40)]\times1{,}000}{31.1035}
=\frac{30{,}400}{31.1035}
=\boxed{977.382\text{ koz Au}}
\]

**(b) Ore Reserve**
\[
\frac{[(4.5\times1.70)+(7.5\times1.30)]\times1{,}000}{31.1035}
=\frac{17{,}400}{31.1035}
=\boxed{559.423\text{ koz Au}}
\]

**(c) Reserve contained gold as a percentage of Measured plus Indicated contained gold**
\[
\frac{17.4}{30.4}\times100=\boxed{57.237\%}
\]

This is a contained-metal comparison, not a metallurgical recovery or a demonstrated Resource-to-Reserve conversion rate. The Resource and Reserve have different reporting bases.

**(d)** Present two separate statements:

- **Mineral Resources:** Measured, Indicated and Inferred categories separately, at the stated **0.5 g/t Au cut-off**, explicitly labelled **inclusive of Ore Reserves**. Inferred is excluded from calculation (a).
- **Ore Reserves:** Proved and Probable categories separately, explicitly identified as **after dilution and reported at the plant-feed reference point**.

Do not publish an additive Resource-plus-Reserve total. Nor should an exclusive Resource be calculated by simply subtracting diluted plant-feed Reserve tonnes from Resource tonnes. Reserve contained gold is not recovered or saleable gold; metallurgical recovery would still need to be applied.

