**The most important judgement is that the resource table fails metal balance: using different densities for tonnage and grade inflates contained gold by 29.73%.**

(a) Directly from volume and volumetric grade:

\[
21{,}000{,}000\ \mathrm{m^3}\times0.32\ \mathrm{g/m^3}
=6{,}720{,}000\ \mathrm{g\ Au}
\]

\[
\text{Contained gold}=\frac{6{,}720{,}000}{31.1035\times1{,}000}
=\boxed{216.052856\ \mathrm{koz}}
\]

(b) From the table, retaining the unrounded grade:

\[
\text{Grade}=\frac{0.32}{1.85}=0.17297297297\ \mathrm{g/t}
\]

\[
\text{Contained gold}
=\frac{50{,}400{,}000\times(0.32/1.85)}{31.1035\times1{,}000}
=\boxed{280.284786\ \mathrm{koz}}
\]

(c) Percentage difference:

\[
\frac{(b)-(a)}{(a)}\times100
=\left(\frac{2.4}{1.85}-1\right)\times100
=\boxed{29.729730\%}
\]

(d) The conversion is invalid. A laboratory density measured on competent quartzite pebbles does not establish the **bulk density of undisturbed gravel**, which includes interparticle voids, matrix and moisture. More fundamentally, applying 2.4 t/m³ to tonnes and 1.85 t/m³ to grade creates gold arithmetically.

Using 1.85 consistently would produce:

\[
21.0\ \mathrm{million\ m^3}\times1.85
=38.85\ \mathrm{Mt}
\]

At \(0.32/1.85\) g/t, this preserves **216.052856 koz**. However, these are wet tonnes. Representative in-situ gravel bulk-density measurements and moisture determination are needed to establish the appropriate reporting basis; 1.85 t/m³ cannot automatically be adopted as dry bulk density.

Under JORC (2012), the Competent Person requires qualifying professional membership and **at least five years of relevant experience** in the mineralisation style, deposit type and activity undertaken. For this estimate, relevant competence must cover alluvial gold sampling and resource estimation, including coarse-gold variability, pitting representativity, gravel geometry and bulk-density measurement. Hard-rock gold experience alone does not establish that competence. Correcting the conversion also does not establish resource classification or reasonable prospects for eventual economic extraction.

